00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:31.330
GANG CHEN: OK, so
let's get started.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:31.930
A recap.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.020
You should be all pleased
that you have learned a lot.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.340
We covered the
quantum, solid state,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.360
and the statistical physics in--

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.810
this is my lecture 11.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:54.540
And so what we learned, first,
the quantum and the solid state

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.140
gives us the energy levels
and the energy state--

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.430
what is the quantum state.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.620
And the statistics give us at
the equilibrium, which quantum

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.980
state, how many particles--

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.400
phonons, electrons, photons.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.180
And going back to counting
the state degeneratively,

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.990
we get the density of state.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.860
So at a certain energy
level per unit energy

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.150
interval or per unit,
we factor how many

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.950
quantum states we have.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.690
So with that, you
can do the counting.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:32.360
And this is a F times
D gives me number

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.510
of particles near the
energy per energy interval.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.190
If you integrate,
that should give you

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:42.690
the total number of particles--
electrons, phonons, photons.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.290
Or, if you multiply by
the energy of a particle,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.540
that gives you the energy.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.040
Or, if you multiply
by charge, it'll

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.110
give you the charge density.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.740
If you multiply by the momentum,
it gives you momentum, right?

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:02.050
So with that, you can
start doing, for example,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:06.660
the energy stored,
specific heat, et cetera.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.820
And then we said that, at
the end of last lecture,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.080
we're moving on to
look at the transport.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.590
And the first idea
is we look at is,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:22.440
if I want to find out how much
energy is going from 0.1 to 0.2

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.780
into different space, and
what I'm going to find out

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.420
is, what's the transmittance?

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.600
So if I have a
particle or wave, now I

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.370
don't know how to call it.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.710
It's particle-wave duality.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:43.950
And say if I have a carrier,
electron, phonon, photon

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.800
launching from point 1, and
the transmittance is how much,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.570
how many of them will
reach point two, right?

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.230
That will give me the fraction.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:59.190
And then say, if I
know the velocity,

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.490
then I know the flux
from 0.1 to 0.2, which

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.700
is the velocity in that
direction, transmittance,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.290
and if I want to find the
energy flux, that's per, say,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.640
this is how much energy I
have starting from that point,

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.360
right?

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.690
So this is where we're going.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.750
In the common
chapter, we're going

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.590
to focus on using the
wave picture to deal

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.600
with this transmittance.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.260
And then, if I want to find
the left flux, if you recall,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.930
in chapter 1, we
derived Fourier law.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.870
We say from 1 to 2
and minus 2 to what?

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:41.040
The difference can
be the left flux.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:48.330
So what we're emphasizing so
far is how to do the counting.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:56.380
And our focus in this chapter is
talking about the wave picture,

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:56.880
right?

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.850
We've been using the web
and Schrodinger's equation

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.810
